00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.203
So what is continuous
integration?

00:00:56.203 --> 00:00:58.370
You guys probably already
have a brief understanding

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.037
of what that is.

00:00:59.037 --> 00:01:02.390
But just to rehash it, it
involves developers integrating

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.960
code as often as possible
within a shared repository

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.170
and the process around that,
including automating, building,

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.850
and testing for
that application.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:15.580
It allows teams to get feedback
quickly and find issues early.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.090
And then it can be
extended further

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.570
after you've implemented CI
into either something called

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.568
continuous delivery or
continuous deployment,

00:01:22.568 --> 00:01:24.110
where those changes
are automatically

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.330
deployed into various
environment levels.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.230
So on the screen here now,
we have a sample application

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.270
infrastructure on
what something could

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.150
look like that
incorporates Kubernetes.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.910
You'll notice that the
important components here

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.860
are the implementation
of the Container

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.370
Registry for hosting
Docker images and GitHub

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.370
for your source control.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:52.320
So briefly about
Kubernetes, at a glance

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.760
this is sort of
the representation

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.240
of the architecture
of Kubernetes.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.510
It helps you manage
container clusters,

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.520
supports multi-cloud,
bare metal environments,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.870
and supports multiple
container runtimes.

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:04.620
There are several also
additional benefits

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.140
you get out of running
GKE on Google Cloud,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.760
including auto-scaling
and auto-healing.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.540
One of the components of
Kubernetes is the API server.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.450
So this is basically how you
do all of your interactions

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.000
with the Kubernetes
cluster and is essentially

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.670
how the CI tool is going to
interface with the cluster

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.830
as well.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.340
It's all API-centric.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.580
There's no private APIs.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.620
There's no system-only call.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.680
Everything communicates
through the API.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.270
And it's a RESTful
type of service.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.920
So you see, we have an example
of that you can actually

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.690
see on the bottom
right hand side,

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.580
although you can't
really read it.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.175
The detailed logs that
you get in Stackdriver

00:02:44.175 --> 00:02:46.050
are showing all the API
calls that are being

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:49.820
made as part of a deployment.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.570
Controllers are fundamental
to how Kubernetes work.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.240
It's a piece of code that
watches a Kubernetes API

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.950
and reacts accordingly.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.310
Essentially, it's code
that runs in a loop,

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.700
listens to the API server,
and responds to changes.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.200
For example, when
you want to create

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.570
a pod, what you really want
is to schedule something

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:09.170
in the system.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:10.830
The scheduler wakes
up in the system,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.220
determines where it's
going to put in the pod,

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.910
and it writes back
to the API server.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.660
The kubelet then wakes
up and orchestrates

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.080
what we want to the system.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.370
So it's all unified together
as part of this process that's

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.980
part of the CI/CD pipeline.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.820
So the nodes, the nodes are
the actual worker machines that

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.790
run the containers, manage
the master components,

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.250
including services like the
container runtime, the kubelet,

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:35.930
and the kube-proxy.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:37.860
If you're running it
in on-prem environment,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:38.990
this would be a
physical machine.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:40.990
If you're running in the
cloud, it's most likely

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.020
going to be a virtual machine.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:46.550
The container runtime
has a interface

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.350
which actually
facilitates pluggability,

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:50.630
supporting different types
of containers, including

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.430
Docker, rkt, and others.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.370
The kubelet is the most
prominent controller

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.470
which implements the
pod and the node APIs

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.080
to drive the container
execution layer.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.780
And the kube-proxy is
a service abstraction

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:06.160
that groups pods under
a common access policy.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.960
It programs the IP table
rules to trap access

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.717
to service virtual
IPs and redirect them

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:11.800
to the appropriate backup.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.000
The pods are small groups
of containers and volumes.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.000
They're tightly coupled.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.542
They live and die together, as
they're spun up and spun down.

00:04:22.542 --> 00:04:25.260
And they have shared
networking and lifecycle.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.430
The containers within a
pod share an IP address,

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.140
and they find each
other via localhost.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.700
Now, a pod typically is set up
to run just a single container.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.937
But it can run
multiple if necessary,

00:04:37.937 --> 00:04:39.020
depending on the use case.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.060
Deployments are the declarative
updates for the pod.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.002
So basically, you
specify a desired state.

00:04:46.002 --> 00:04:48.460
The deployment controller then
handles the actual execution

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.480
of reaching that state.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.230
Services are an
abstraction that defines

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:56.900
the logical set of
pods and the policy

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.350
by which they are accessed.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:04:59.910
Each pod has an IP.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.190
Services provide a
stable virtual IP,

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.460
so that even as you
scale up and down as part

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.340
of your process for
auto-scaling or any issues

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:12.390
with self-healing, it
maintains a single virtual IP

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.490
that you can use to
access those services.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.310
And finally, secrets
and ConfigMaps,

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.885
these hold all the sensitive
information, such as passwords,

00:05:22.885 --> 00:05:25.575
OAuth tokens, et cetera.

00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:27.450
This is obviously going
to be a key component

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.780
of the CI/CD process for
maintaining the secrets that

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:35.880
are going to be distributed
across each environment.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:37.540
There are numerous
automatic secrets

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:39.832
that are created by Kubernetes
to store the credentials

00:05:39.832 --> 00:05:41.010
to access the API.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.750
They are base64
encoded, not encrypted.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.380
The ConfigMaps are
then used to store

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:47.880
the nonsensitive information.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.560
And then you have
an imagePullSecrets

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.840
that's utilized to set
Container Registry credentials.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:54.540
That's going to
be crucial when we

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.240
talk about the implementation
of the CI/CD pipeline

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.530
and how it interfaces.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.475
Kubectl is obviously
the main CLI

00:06:04.475 --> 00:06:06.600
that you're going to utilize
to execute any actions

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:07.475
against the cluster--

00:06:07.475 --> 00:06:10.050
listing pods, applying
changes, things of that nature.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.160
This is another key
component that's

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:15.900
going to be utilized
by our CI tool

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.530
as part of the build and
for the CI/CD component

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:20.610
for the deployment.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.280
So now, we get into
orchestration tools.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:30.000
These are key components
of the CI/CD process

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.180
that are utilized in the
actual automation pipeline.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:37.110
So GCP service accounts
are going to be crucial.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:40.290
Everything should be running
through a service account,

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.180
rather than a user account.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.360
You'll be managing those
directly, typically

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.560
either within some form of a
deployment management solution

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:53.340
or in the console or
even via the gcloud CLI.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:00.010
Deployment Manager is a
solution that's typically

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:03.910
used Google native around
automating the cloud

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:08.230
deployment, so that you have
your infrastructure codified.

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:11.530
It's repeatable, declarative.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:15.010
The templates can also be
pulled once created manually

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:16.090
for resource recreation.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:19.280
So you can reuse that across
multiple different services.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.720
Now, Terraform is often a
common alternative to this.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:23.930
That's also useful.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:25.983
They both have their drawbacks.

00:07:25.983 --> 00:07:27.400
In this particular
instance, we're

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:32.950
talking about a GCP Deployment
Manager implementation.

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:38.170
For automation, we are
utilizing Travis CI.

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:41.470
It's basically a hosted
CI tool for handling

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:43.137
all of the automation.

00:07:43.137 --> 00:07:44.470
All of our scripts are in there.

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:48.610
So it handles everything from
downloading the gcloud SDK;

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:51.280
setting the appropriate
zone, cluster, and project

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.850
to deploy to; activating
a service account;

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.410
and installing the kubectl CLI.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:59.080
Obviously, CircleCI is another
alternative, Cloud Build,

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:02.050
Jenkins, any tool.

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:04.900
We actually use a combination
of both Travis and Circle

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:06.247
in our implementation.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:12.440
For our artifact repository,
we're utilizing Artifactory.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.250
It's a universal
artifact repository.

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:16.010
It's been around for
a while, supports

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:19.230
many software packages
created in any language,

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:21.830
CI/CD integration with
nearly every provider,

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:23.840
and Hook Properties
for simple querying

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.720
when you need to
access specific items.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.120
It handles all of these
different multiple repository

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:32.169
types, including Docker,
NPM, Maven, Gradle,

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:35.480
Debian, and generic repository.

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.870
So those listed out are key,
because those are actually

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:42.110
the big components as part
of our Kubernetes CI/CD

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:44.220
deployment.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.910
The generic artifact
repository, in this case,

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:50.210
is utilized for the Android APK.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:53.360
That allows us to build and test
and publish internally prior

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:54.680
to leasing to the App Store.

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.480
Also note, in this
implementation

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.340
we discuss potentially
utilizing Helm.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.440
In our implementation,
we do not yet use that,

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:06.080
but it is a recommended
use case and would

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:09.245
be useful in the future to
be hosted in Artifactory.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:14.575
So the build pipeline.

00:09:14.575 --> 00:09:16.200
This is where we talk
about essentially

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:19.440
how it's all orchestrated.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.390
In our particular
build pipeline,

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.950
the application
code is decoupled

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:26.350
from the infrastructure code
in separate repositories.

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:28.980
So we have two different
repositories for each service

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:31.290
that we're deploying.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:34.830
The deployments are orchestrated
on these various environments.

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:38.610
Or specific
containers are, again,

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.770
completely disassociated
from the application code.

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:42.830
So the process at a
really high level--

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:45.060
and we'll walk through
that deeper later--

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:48.900
is that the application code is
built, compiled, and packaged.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.120
Code is pushed to the
binary repository, which

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.120
houses binaries of all
sorts, including the NPM

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:57.600
modules, any jars, Docker
images, and any other binaries

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:58.740
that might be needed.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.240
And then upon
deployment, the binary

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.210
is pulled from the repository
and consumed as part of another

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.480
build orchestration
step or deployed

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:07.200
to the appropriate resource.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:09.000
It's fairly modular
and flexible.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.910
Note that we can actually build
and orchestrate deployments

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:14.850
across individual services
or across the environment

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:16.010
holistically.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:17.700
I'll walk through that in a bit.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:24.190
So part of how Artifactory
is used on GCP,

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:25.750
we push the images
from the build.

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:27.520
Obviously during
the build step, we

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:30.490
create a Docker image that's
pushed to Artifactory.

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:32.590
It can also be pushed to GCR.

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:35.180
That's Google
Container Repository.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:36.833
Note that that
GCR implementation

00:10:36.833 --> 00:10:38.500
is a really good
choice, because there's

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:41.950
native support within GKE.

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:43.990
In the case we are using
a private repository,

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:46.840
such as we are in this
instance, we need to create

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:51.040
an imagePullSecrets link with a
Secrets config type using that

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:57.780
kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.370
Next, we update the container
image through the pipeline.

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:02.700
So as I stated, you're
essentially seeing on the right

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:07.680
there an image of the source
config for the infrastructure

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.440
configuration, again in
the independent repository.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:13.270
So actually, we've gone through
now, we've created the image.

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:16.260
It's been tested and
validated and successful.

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.750
We update the Docker
image tag in that config.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.940
Finally, we apply the
changes to the cluster.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:26.860
Just one quick note,
all of these items

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:29.043
are actually automated
through build scripts.

00:11:29.043 --> 00:11:31.210
I'll show you a little bit
what that looks like when

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:32.440
I get to the demo section.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:35.050
But none of this is
actually done by hand.

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:39.950
Now, we actually
have an overview

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:43.070
of this sort of
cohesive, automated test,

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:45.120
publish, and deploy pipeline.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.780
So starting from the top
left, all of our code

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:51.440
is hosted in GitHub, or whatever
repository you might utilize.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:53.240
It works the same way.

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:55.760
That then, based
on a pull request,

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:59.600
will lead towards a build
on Travis, which then pulls

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.360
the code, builds the containers,
any associated artifacts that

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:05.720
are necessary, runs the
test on a test environment,

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:08.360
and publishes those artifacts
to a binary repository,

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:10.760
in this case Artifactory.

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:12.830
Those containers
are then, as you

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:14.330
can see on the
bottom right, they're

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:17.090
labeled with the
appropriate version numbers.

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.910
This implementation, we have
a microservices approach.

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:21.470
We have several
different services

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:23.660
that are tagged with all
of the different versions

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:25.370
for the multiple
different services

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:29.450
with their own version
tags, their own environment.

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:35.570
labels, as well as
describing the type of code

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:38.950
that we're utilizing
for that implementation.

00:12:38.950 --> 00:12:41.735
Then Travis then makes
a call to deploy this

00:12:41.735 --> 00:12:44.110
to the Kubernetes environment,
which pulls the containers

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:46.210
from that repository.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:49.660
Note also a key thing here is
that monitoring is built in

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:51.760
throughout the entire
process through a number

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:53.180
of different sources.

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:55.870
So we are monitoring,
obviously, via Stackdriver.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:58.660
We are also getting
notifications from GitHub,

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:01.570
as those pull requests are
being approved and succeeding

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:04.480
or failing, similarly
on the Travis side.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:06.573
All of those have hooks,
and in our environment

00:13:06.573 --> 00:13:08.490
are integrated to notify
us via Slack channel.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.150
All that then creates
our full application.

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:15.700
So you see this is
utilized across all

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:19.400
of our different environments
for web, backend, iOS,

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:19.900
and Android.

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:25.880
And so this is a
representation now of basically

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:29.090
our completed
infrastructure that I talked

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:30.570
about we would be implementing.

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:33.560
So you see it utilizes
those shared services

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:37.400
across the multiple clients,
pointing to a load balancer

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:39.650
to Kubernetes Engine.

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:41.870
In this instance,
we're not technically

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:43.470
going to go into
detail of that here.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:45.830
But we have two database
types for representing

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:49.080
different types of data.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:50.760
We also have cloud storage.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:53.760
And the key factor, again,
here is at the bottom.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.450
We have the representation
of the binary repository

00:13:56.450 --> 00:13:59.090
being Artifactory and our
source control being GitHub.

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:06.650
So the continuous
integration dev pipeline.

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:10.920
So this is essentially
how we get our code

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:15.660
through to the integration
steps all the way to deployed

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:18.520
in our dev environment.

00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:20.130
I'll walk you through that.

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:23.640
So essentially, we
have our source code.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:25.890
In this case, I'm going to
follow the source code path

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:29.290
for our front end application.

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:33.705
So our application source
code is in our master branch.

00:14:33.705 --> 00:14:35.580
For that to work then
we have a pull request.

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:36.330
That's created.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:37.560
It's reviewed by our team.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:38.760
It's approved.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:44.010
Code is then merged into master
with a new application version.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:46.020
That new commit triggers
a build on the master

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.510
branch in Travis CI.

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:50.730
Then we see Travis
CI making the call

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:52.500
to now successfully
build a new image,

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:54.630
pushing it into Artifactory.

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.420
Once that checks out, we
move on to the next stage,

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.320
where it checks out the dev
branch in the associated pod

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:02.350
repository.

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:03.810
So in this case,
the pod repository

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.000
is that independent
infrastructure repository

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:10.020
we're using separately from
our application source code.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:14.150
And then it updates a deployment
container in an image.

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:17.580
The changes to the
deployment.yaml configuration

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:21.950
are then committed to the pod
repository in the dev branch.

00:15:21.950 --> 00:15:25.980
Then you see down there, we
have the web pod repository.

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:27.590
The dev branch has
then been updated.

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:30.680
And that commit triggers
a new build process--

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:34.030
next step in the build
process, which builds

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:37.700
the dev branch in Travis CI.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:41.400
That process is actually
the deployment step--

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:45.262
so utilizing a combination
of gcloud and the service

00:15:45.262 --> 00:15:47.720
accounts we mentioned earlier,
as well as several different

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:50.750
build scripts that are part
of the setup of that pod.

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:53.960
It successfully deploys
the resource configurations

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:56.600
to the Kubernetes resource.

00:15:56.600 --> 00:16:00.740
Once that's done, the image on
the Kubernetes side, the images

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:02.810
will then be pulled
into that deployment.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:05.900
And rolling updates are made to
the underlying infrastructure.

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:14.820
So the next step is the
continuous integration

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:17.670
for a single or multiple
container promotion.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:19.950
So this would be if we
want to just update--

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.880
again, I'm talking
in this instance

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:24.760
about a microservices-based
environment.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:30.240
So if we wanted to just
update a single service,

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:32.320
we would follow this step.

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:39.270
So here, you see we had the
backend pod for the dev branch.

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:41.920
In order to promote to
QA in this instance,

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:45.450
a pull request gets open
against the QA branch.

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:47.250
That pull request,
once approved,

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:51.030
then updates a single
or multiple image tags,

00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:52.530
depending on the
configuration we're

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:57.840
choosing, in the deployment.yaml
to the versions in dev.

00:16:57.840 --> 00:16:59.250
Then it's reviewed and approved.

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:02.320
And then code is merged
into the QA branch.

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.490
Once that happens,
that triggers a new

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:08.095
build on Travis
CI, again running

00:17:08.095 --> 00:17:09.720
through a similar
process to go through

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.940
and actually execute
the build stages

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.548
and then deploy to
the cloud environment.

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:21.869
So this gives you sort
of a representation

00:17:21.869 --> 00:17:25.050
of how the releases can be
attributed from one environment

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:25.660
to the next.

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:27.660
So we have multiple
different versions

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:32.880
of each service in
dev, UAT, and prod.

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:35.560
We have an overarching version
for the environment level.

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:38.610
That's noted at the bottom.

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:41.400
In this instance, the
front end and backend

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.160
are different pods altogether.

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:45.990
These pods have a
series of containers

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:47.010
that describe the pods.

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:49.200
So going from one
environment to the next

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:51.745
involves kicking off
a CI build process

00:17:51.745 --> 00:17:53.370
that reaches out to
the deployment.yaml

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:55.290
for that specific
environment and applies it

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:57.450
on the appropriate cluster.

00:17:57.450 --> 00:17:59.940
This can be orchestrated with
either different branches

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:01.245
or different folders.

00:18:01.245 --> 00:18:03.870
In this particular instance, we
are using the brand segregation

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:05.415
for different environments.

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:12.160
So this is the in-depth
process around how

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:16.150
we do the continuous integration
for environment-to-environment

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:16.660
promotion.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:19.840
So when we want to take
all of these services

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:21.520
from one environment
to the next,

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:24.820
being promoted from, in
this instance, QA to UAT,

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:27.890
this is that workflow.

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.827
So it starts off
with we actually--

00:18:30.827 --> 00:18:32.410
so in order for this
workflow to work,

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:35.560
we have a separate
environments repository that

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:38.290
stores the status of each
particular environment,

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:41.470
again using that branch
segregation methodology.

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:46.030
So it has a JSON schema
representing each pod,

00:18:46.030 --> 00:18:48.550
as well as the underlying
containers of that pod.

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:50.170
That represents
the current version

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:52.370
of that pod in that environment.

00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:56.980
So this environments repository,
we look at the UAT branch,

00:18:56.980 --> 00:18:59.350
again similar processes before.

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:02.410
A pull request is
created from QA to UAT.

00:19:02.410 --> 00:19:04.750
That pull request
is then approved.

00:19:04.750 --> 00:19:08.190
Code is merged into UAT
with the versions from QA.

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:10.900
And then once that
happens, that new commit

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:13.650
triggers a build on the
UAT branch in Travis CI.

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:20.410
Then in Travis, it
checks out the UAT branch

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:22.960
in every pod defined based
on that environment JSON

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:25.190
configuration I mentioned.

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:27.880
Every image in the pod
repository UAT branch

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:31.390
is an updated to
reflect the version that

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:35.440
was mentioned, that JSON config
for the requisite version

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:36.760
of that pod.

00:19:36.760 --> 00:19:39.040
The changes to each
pod's deployment.yaml

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:41.980
are then committed to the UAT
branch of that repository.

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:44.230
So it orchestrates
those changes across all

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:47.672
of the different repositories
for each service.

00:19:47.672 --> 00:19:49.130
Each of these new
commits trigger a

00:19:49.130 --> 00:19:55.340
build in the pod UAT branch in
Travis CI, that then actually,

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:58.340
again, goes through that same
process, builds the container,

00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:01.070
makes all the appropriate
configuration change request,

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:03.263
and pushes that
through to Kubernetes.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:08.400
So next, just to get you a
little bit of a representation

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:10.590
of what this looks
like, I'm going

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:12.720
to switch to this demo
to sort of see basically

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:15.380
what our source
repositories look like.

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:20.350
So as I mentioned, I'll start
with the environment JSON

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:21.100
configuration.

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:25.300
So as I stated, we have
an independent repository

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:26.380
called environments.

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:28.470
Within that, we have
an environment.json

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:31.960
that has the appropriate
configuration

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:35.103
values for each pod
in that environment.

00:20:35.103 --> 00:20:36.520
So we have a ton
of different pods

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:39.070
with a ton of
different containers.

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:41.130
Again, we're using this
branch methodology.

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:44.128
So we switch between
dev, prod, qa, and uat.

00:20:44.128 --> 00:20:46.420
And those will also have
different configuration values

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:48.310
based on the respective
version that's being

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:49.793
utilized in that environment.

00:20:53.700 --> 00:20:56.510
The other point to
represent, as I stated,

00:20:56.510 --> 00:20:57.760
we have separate repositories.

00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:02.250
So our source code in this
case for the web application

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:05.110
is all hosted in this
single repository.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:07.620
And then we also have pod
configurations, again,

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:10.570
as represented in the
independent repository.

00:21:10.570 --> 00:21:12.540
You'll notice, obviously,
the source code

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:14.582
is where all the devs are
going to do their work.

00:21:14.582 --> 00:21:16.800
The pod is, in
this case, managed

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:21.150
and monitored by a headless
user that Travis CI uses.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:27.260
And as I stated,
that will then update

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:29.110
with the appropriate
image as I represented

00:21:29.110 --> 00:21:31.610
in that earlier slide as well
with the correct configuration

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:33.650
value.

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:37.040
Another key point
to be aware of,

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:39.410
as I stated, all of these
tasks that I'm representing

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:42.910
that are happening within
the CI are all scripted out

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:45.470
in our application.

00:21:45.470 --> 00:21:47.510
In this case, because we
have so many services,

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:49.280
they all share
similar configurations

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:52.700
in how the CI process works and
how we plan on utilizing it.

00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:55.910
We have a common CI repository.

00:21:55.910 --> 00:21:57.590
That forms the basis
for all the scripts

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:59.215
that we utilize across
our application.

00:21:59.215 --> 00:22:01.840
